    "case_id": "400",
    "case_summary": "A man in his late 60s with a 15-year history of Parkinson disease (Hoehn and Yahr score, 3) and 20-year history of sleep disturbance presented with worsening dream enactment behavior. While the patient's daytime motor symptoms improved following deep brain stimulation, episodes of dream enactment became progressively more violent, resulting in assault on his wife and self-injurious behaviors. Yelling occurred multiple times per night. Violent behaviors were reported every other night, such as punching through walls or striking furniture.\n\nDespite removing all objects from the bedroom and laying the mattress onto the floor, the patient continued to strike out at walls and the ground, causing self-injury and damage to property during sleep episodes. One severe episode resulted in a head laceration after striking the wall, requiring emergency department evaluation.\n\n- Initial polysomnography demonstrated obstructive sleep apnea (OSA), with an Apnea Hypopnea Index score of 8.5.  - Emergency department neuroimaging was unremarkable.  - Repeat polysomnography demonstrated an Apnea Hypopnea Index score of 12.8.    Figure Title: Rapid Eye Movement Behavior Disorder and Violent Events",
